"Not held in other editions"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/889"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Longest distance travelled (#2), Handicap, Open" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "9 – 10 September 1900"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic (non-medal)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/850"} "L'Aérostation, Parc de Vincennes, Paris"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/925101"} "13 from 1 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There were 26 ballooning events at the 1900 Paris Exposition, which were more properly termed exhibitions. They were mixed, with both men and women participating. The events all took place starting at the Aérostation, at the Parc de Vincennes, in Paris."] "\n\n" [:p {} "There were four balloon events decided by greatest distance travelled: " [:a {:href "/results/925102"} "one open"] ", two with handicap, and one termed a " [:a {:href "/results/925106"} "second degree event"] ", which was only open to prize winners in the first three events. This handicap event was won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/890433"} "Henry, Count de la Vaulx"] ", who competed in 19 balloon events at the 1900 Paris Exposition and won six of them, including the all-around competition, the " [:a {:href "/results/925096"} "Grand Prix de l’aéronautique"] ", and three of the four distance travelled events. For this win, he received a gilded plaque valued at 500 francs."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Count de la Vaulx was considered the pioneer of aeronautics in France, and was a co-founder of the Aero Club de France in 1898. Later in October 1900, in the second degree event, he and " [:a {:href "/athletes/890261"} "Georges Castillon de Saint-Victor"] " set a distance record for the most distance travelled by balloon, covering just over 1,925 km (1,195 miles) from Vincennes to Korostychev, near Kyiv, Ukraine, in 35¾ hours. He was given the Grand Medal of the 